      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "Knee flexion contractures occur frequently in non-ambulatory, aged persons and persons with central nervous system lesions, rendering positioning and nursing care difficult. There are often risks associated with surgical interventions."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "To evaluate the effectiveness of percutaneous needle tenotomy to lengthen the knee flexor muscles and improve passive function."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "This was a retrospective study of all patients who underwent percutaneous needle tenotomy between 2012 and 2014. Tenotomy was carried out in the semi-tendinosus, biceps femoris and gracillis muscles under local anesthesia. The procedure took no more than 40 minutes. Range of motion (ROM) was evaluated immediately post-operatively and 3 months later."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Thirty-four needle tenotomies were carried out. Mean lack of knee extension was 94.2° (range 35-120°) pre-op, (range 15-90°; p<0.05) immediately post-op and 50.1° (range 10-90°; p<0.05) three months later, thus a mean increase of 44.1° knee extension (range 0-90°). All care and positioning objectives were achieved. There were no complications and procedure-related pain was rated as 3-4/ 10."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "Needle tenotomy was well tolerated and yielded a significant increase in ROM with no unwanted effects. All objectives were achieved. This technique could be used in an ambulatory care setting or within institutions for severely disabled individuals."
        }
      ]},
